<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Associated Domains, which is what lets iOS run a passkey ceremony
	  for this app.

	  A passkey is bound to a DOMAIN, not to an app. iOS runs the
	  ceremony only once it has verified that this app may act for
	  iakauntan.com, and it verifies that in two halves:

	    * this entitlement, the app's half, naming the domain it claims;
	    * and /.well-known/apple-app-site-association on that domain,
	      the domain's half, naming the apps it admits by team id and
	      bundle id.

	  Both are required. With only this one, iOS refuses and the app
	  says "not set up for passkeys on this system yet". The sentence is
	  passkeyNotSetUpHere, in
	  lib/src/features/auth/passkey_failure.dart.

	  webcredentials: and not applinks:. They are different services on
	  the same mechanism. applinks decides which URLs open the app;
	  webcredentials decides which passwords and passkeys are shared
	  with it. Only the second is wanted, and asking for the first would
	  make this app open every link on the site.

	  ## A prerequisite outside this repository

	  Signing with this entitlement requires the App ID
	  my.iakauntan.iakauntan to have the Associated Domains capability
	  enabled in the Apple Developer portal, under Certificates,
	  Identifiers and Profiles. Without it the provisioning profile does
	  not carry the entitlement and the SIGNED build fails at signing.

	  CI is unaffected. ci.yml builds iOS with codesigning off, because
	  that job answers "does it compile" and holds no signing identity.
	  The first place this can fail is a real release build.

	  ## During development

	  Appending ?mode=developer to the domain bypasses Apple's CDN,
	  which caches the association file for about a day and will
	  otherwise keep serving a 404 long after the file is deployed. It
	  must not ship: a build carrying it does not validate against the
	  real service.

	  (No double hyphen appears in this comment, and that is deliberate:
	  XML forbids one inside a comment, and the same mistake in
	  core/xlsx.dart produced a stylesheet no reader would parse.)
	-->
	<key>com.apple.developer.associated-domains</key>
	<array>
		<string>webcredentials:iakauntan.com</string>
	</array>

	<!--
	  Push Notifications, which is what lets AppDelegate.swift call
	  registerForRemoteNotifications() and receive a real APNs token
	  rather than didFailToRegisterForRemoteNotificationsWithError.

	  development here, not production: Xcode requires this string to
	  match the provisioning profile a signed build uses, and the only
	  builds this repository can currently produce are development
	  ones, since there is no App Store or TestFlight build yet (see
	  docs/handoff.md, "blocked on the user"). A distribution build's
	  own profile carries production, and Xcode's automatic signing
	  changes this value to match at archive time when it does; this is
	  the value for every build this project's own CI or a developer's
	  machine can actually produce today, not a claim about what a
	  future release build will use.

	  CI is unaffected the same way the domain entitlement above is:
	  ci.yml builds with codesigning off, so this value is never
	  checked against a profile there. The first place a mismatch can
	  be caught is a real signed build.

	  (No double hyphen appears in this comment, and that is
	  deliberate, for the same reason the note above this one gives.)
	-->
	<key>aps-environment</key>
	<string>development</string>
</dict>
</plist>
